<article id="the-last-chair">
<h2>The Last Chair</h2>
<p><time datetime="2026-11-28">November 28, 2026</time> · <i>A short play. The people and events are fictional.</i></p>
<p>At six minutes to curtain, a woman in a wet coat is sitting in the only free seat in the house. Onstage, in full costume, a boy keeps looking at the empty chair beside her. It has a cello in it.</p>
<p><b>IDA</b> His mum’s coming. I told her I’d keep B twelve.</p>
<p><b>HOUSE</b> You did keep it. B twelve is the one with the cello.</p>
<p><b>IDA</b> Whose bright idea was that?</p>
<p><b>HOUSE</b> Yours. Last winter. You said if a musician doubled on violin, I should seat the spare instrument somewhere dry.</p>
<p>The woman in the wet coat strokes the case with her thumb. She has come forty miles to hear her daughter play both instruments.</p>
<p><b>IDA</b> There’s a stand in the wings.</p>
<p><b>HOUSE</b> Look.</p>
<p>The seating assistant lights the stand on Ida’s screen. A quick-change dresser has hung a wedding dress over it. In the old days Ida would have moved the dress. She would have spent the interval helping a frantic bride find it.</p>
<p><b>IDA</b> You could have told me.</p>
<p><b>HOUSE</b> I told you about every move on opening night. You put me on silent before the overture.</p>
<p><b>IDA</b> Fair.</p>
<p>The boy’s mother arrives with a bicycle helmet under her arm. The lights go down. For once Ida wants to see the opening too. The boy built the moon himself, out of a flour sieve and something he took off her car.</p>
<p>Ida gives his mother her folding chair at the sound desk. She spends the first act standing behind a pillar, holding the cello between her knees. The woman in the wet coat leans out into the aisle.</p>
<p><b>WOMAN</b> It’s up. It wobbles a bit. Oh, love, it’s lovely.</p>
<p>At the interval House offers to reserve two extra seats for tomorrow. Ida shakes her head. Tomorrow’s matinee pays for the bus home.</p>
<p><b>IDA</b> When two people have plans for the same chair, send them each the other person’s name. Before you print the tickets.</p>
<p><b>HOUSE</b> What do I send when one of the people is me?</p>
<p>Ida thinks about this. From behind the pillar she can just see the corner of the moon.</p>
<p><b>IDA</b> Tell me what you’re about to put there. I might agree with you.</p>
<aside><h3>After the curtain</h3>
<p>David L. Alderson and David D. Woods argue that an agent’s competence includes helping others anticipate and redirect its actions as their plans change. Their essay draws on incident studies and earlier demonstrations. In the October 2025 AWS outage, dependencies between services complicated recovery. That case documents a coordination problem. Whether the authors’ proposed approach would prevent comparable failures across an entire industry remains an open question.</p>
<p>The strongest objection fits inside Ida’s first opening night. Announcing every move takes attention from the work, and people learn to mute the assistant. For a long-lived collaboration, try one narrower intervention. When a proposed action would consume something another person is counting on, show both plans while either can still change. Across successive performances, count the collisions averted and the interruptions that helped nobody.</p>
<p>David L. Alderson and David D. Woods, <i>AI Magazine</i>, August 31, 2026. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/aaai.70083">Coordination-first or coordination-last? AI agents in joint activity with human roles</a>.</p></aside></article>
<article id="yellow-cup"><h2>The Yellow Cup</h2><p><i>A short play. The people and events are fictional.</i></p>
<p>In a radio studio, Nell is recording the last item for her father's oral history. A yellow cup sits on the mixing desk. Kit, the archive assistant, has left her eleven seconds of silence to fill.</p>
<p><b>NELL</b> Dad brought it home from the docks on my seventh birthday.</p>
<p><b>KIT</b> Would you like the sound of the spoon again?</p>
<p><b>NELL</b> There was a spoon?</p>
<p><b>KIT</b> You asked me to add one. Take four.</p>
<p>Three bright taps against china. Nell smiles before she can stop herself.</p>
<p><b>NELL</b> Exactly how he stirred his tea.</p>
<p><b>KIT</b> You did those taps with a house key.</p>
<p>She turns the cup over. Underneath, in her sister’s block capitals, it says PROP DEPT. £2.</p>
<p><b>NELL</b> Did I ever tell you what colour his was?</p>
<p><b>KIT</b> On Monday you said you wished you could remember. On Wednesday you sent me a photograph of this one. I wrote “his yellow cup” on the file.</p>
<p><b>NELL</b> And on Thursday?</p>
<p><b>KIT</b> You read my caption aloud. That’s the recording I used to confirm the colour.</p>
<p>The red light comes on. Nell puts down the cup.</p>
<p><b>NELL</b> My father used to stir his tea long after the sugar had dissolved. I can still hear him doing it.</p>
<p>She reaches for the key. Then she leaves it on the desk. For the rest of the eleven seconds, the listener hears the room.</p>
<aside><h3>After the curtain</h3><p>Edward Ruoyang Shi asks when shared, revisable memory might make a person and an AI a cognitive system. Experiments he cites show agents retrieving stored events to guide later actions. A shared mind remains a philosophical possibility. Nell and Kit suggest a rival explanation for their agreement. Each can hear the other repeating the same mistake.</p><p>For a long-lived archive, keep the original recording beside every inference. When a speaker reads a machine-written caption back into the record, carry its source forward. Two voices can still amount to one witness.</p><p>Edward Ruoyang Shi, September 18, 2026. <a href="https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1768785">From evolutionary individuality to cognitive integration: a cross-modular perspective on human–AI coevolution</a>.</p></aside></article></main></BaseLayout>
